"text": "Windows 11 comes with a few default settings that make your PC more annoying, less private, and even less safe.\nIn this video, I'll show you nine settings you should change right now, and each one takes less than a minute.\nI'm doing every step on a real Windows PC, so you can follow along click by click.\nAnd stay to the end, because setting number nine stops a popup that has ruined a lot of games.\nSetting number one helps you spot a fake file before you open it.\nBy default, Windows hides the end of every file name, the part called the file extension.\nLook at this file in my Downloads folder.\nIt looks like a normal PDF called invoice.\nTo see the truth, open File Explorer and click View at the top.\nClick Show, then click File name extensions.\nNow the full name appears, and this invoice is really invoice dot pdf dot exe.\nThat means it's a program, not a document.\nNever open a file like this from an email or a website you don't trust.\nWith extensions turned on, you'll always know what you're about to open.\nSetting number two is a faster way to put two windows side by side.\nHere I have Notepad and File Explorer, and they're overlapping.\nClick on the window you want to arrange.\nThen press the Windows key and Z together.\nWindows shows you a set of layouts, and each one has a number.\nPress the number of the layout you want, or just click it.\nWindows snaps the window into place and fills the other side with your open window.\nNow both windows fill the screen perfectly, with no dragging and no resizing.\nThis is great for comparing two documents, or copying from one window into another.\nSetting number three is easier on your eyes, especially at night.\nOpen Settings by pressing Windows and I.\nClick Personalization, then click Colors.\nNext to Choose your mode, open the list.\nPick Dark.\nThe Settings app, File Explorer, the Start menu and the taskbar all switch to dark right away.\nIf you change your mind, pick Light in the same list.\nSetting number four cleans up your Start menu.\nBy default, Windows can fill Start with recently added apps, recent files, tips and suggestions.\nGo to Settings, then Personalization, then Start.\nHere you'll find a group of switches that all start with the word Show.\nTurn off Show recently added apps.\nTurn off Show recommended files in Start, recent files in File Explorer, and items in Jump Lists.\nTurn off Show recommendations for tips, shortcuts, new apps, and more.\nAnd turn off Show account-related notifications.\nOpen Start again, and the recommended area is empty, with only your pinned apps on top.\nQuick favor before setting number five.\nIf these settings are helping you, subscribe to TM Diamond, so you don't miss the next Windows tutorial.\nNow, the next setting is the one most people wish they'd changed years ago.\nSetting number five stops the endless popups.\nGo to Settings, then System, then Notifications.\nTo silence everything for a while, turn on Do not disturb.\nYour notifications still go to the notification center, so you can read them later.\nFor a calmer PC every day, look at the list of apps and other senders below.\nEvery app has its own switch.\nTurn off the switch for any app that keeps interrupting you.\nYou keep the useful ones, and the noisy ones go quiet.\nSetting number six is for anyone who misses the Start button on the left.\nGo to Settings, then Personalization, then Taskbar.\nClick Taskbar behaviors to open more options.\nNext to Taskbar alignment, open the list.\nChange Center to Left.\nThe Start button and your apps move to the left corner, just like older versions of Windows.\nAnd when you open Start, it opens from the left side too.\nSetting number seven shows you the files Windows hides.\nOpen File Explorer and go to your user folder.\nClick View, then Show, then Hidden items.\nNow you can see folders like App Data, where many apps keep their settings.\nHidden items look a little faded, so you can tell them apart.\nThis helps when a guide tells you to find a file inside a hidden folder.\nBe careful here, and don't delete anything in these folders unless you know exactly what it is.\nSetting number eight gives you a little more privacy.\nGo to Settings, then Privacy and security, then General.\nThese switches control your advertising ID, relevant content based on your language list, and tracking of the apps you launch.\nTurn off the switches you don't want.\nOn this PC, I'm turning off the language list and the app launch tracking.\nTurning these off doesn't break anything, and you can turn them back on at any time.\nAnd setting number nine is the one I promised.\nIf you press the Shift key five times, Windows asks if you want to turn on Sticky Keys.\nIn the middle of a game, that popup can be a disaster.\nGo to Settings, then Accessibility, then Keyboard.\nClick Sticky keys.\nTurn off Keyboard shortcut for Sticky keys.\nNow pressing Shift five times won't bring up that popup, and Sticky Keys is still here if you ever need it.\nThat's nine settings that make Windows 11 cleaner, safer and easier to use.\nNext week, I'll show you every safe way to free up disk space on Windows 11, without deleting your files.\nSubscribe to TM Diamond so you don't miss it."
}